  "query_preview": "..A weak dry shortwave moved through this morning with\na few mid level clouds. However, given the light winds at the\nsurface and aloft this passage didn't mix valley inversions out\nvery well. The 12Z sounding showed this inversion well, and\ninversion conditions will likely persist through Thursday before \na more substantial storm moves through the area on Saturday.\n\nSouthwesterly flow will start to increase ahead of an elongated\ntrough on Friday. Current guidance has elevated winds increasing\nat the surface for western portions of the CWA. If they are able\nto break through the surface inversion then hazy conditions in the\nvalleys could see improvement as early as Friday. Otherwise,\nimprovement will likely come on Saturday as widespread valley \nrain and mountain snow move through most of the CWA. Confidence is\nhighest regarding the speed in which this trough moves through.\nMost of the guidance have it moving through relatively quickly\nwhich will limit precip duration and totals. Confidence is still\nshaky regarding the track of this trough. 44% of ensemble members\nkeep the trough to the west with a drier solution that would\nresult in little accumulation of valley rain and mountain snow.\nWhereas the other 56% of members bring a quick round of valley\nrain (0.15-0.4\") and mountain snow (4-8\" above 8000 feet). \n\nA more substantial and potentially colder storm early next week\ncould provide for high precipitation totals and lower snow levels.\nThe clusters are split into two scenarios. The first (67% of\nmembers) scenario brings more of an open wave through the Great \nBasin with widespread valley rain and mountain snow. The second \n(33% chance) scenario has a splitting storm that brings the \ngreatest precipitation to the desert southwest with some \nprecipitation making it into southern Utah. Median 700 mb \ntemperatures dip to -7C with this storm which could bring snow \nlevels as low as 6000-6500 feet.",
